Two significant issues, one of them critical enough that the tree is currently broken for every insert:

**1. Critical — the new node is never linked into the tree.** Compare with the version before the fixup loop was added: after setting up `n` (through [src/rbtree.c:120](src/rbtree.c#L120)), the block that actually attaches it —

```c
if (parent == t->nil)
    t->root = n;
else if (cmp < 0)
    parent->left = n;
else
    parent->right = n;
```

— is gone. `n->parent = parent` is set, but nothing ever sets `parent->left`/`parent->right` (or `t->root` for the first insert) to point at `n`. The dangling `cmp` variable at [src/rbtree.c:89](src/rbtree.c#L89) that's still updated in the search loop but never read afterward is the tell.

Consequences: `n` is heap-allocated but unreachable from `t->root` — `rb_find`/`rb_foreach` will never see it, `rb_destroy` will leak it, and `t->size` becomes wrong relative to what's actually reachable. It also breaks the fixup loop itself: checks like `problemNode == problemNode->parent->left` ([src/rbtree.c:126](src/rbtree.c#L126), [155](src/rbtree.c#L155)) read `parent`'s *stale* left/right pointers, so the triangle-vs-line test can come out wrong even when the coloring logic is right.

This needs to be restored right before the fixup loop starts (line 124).

**2. Bug — the mirror-side Case 3 (lines 160-163) is wrong in three ways** compared to its left-branch counterpart at lines 141-143:

```c
// Case 3
problemNode->color = RB_BLACK;                    // should be problemNode->parent->color
problemNode->parent->parent->color = RB_BLACK;     // should be RB_RED
rb_rotate_right(t, problemNode->parent->parent);   // should be rb_rotate_left
```

It colors `problemNode` black instead of `problemNode->parent`, colors the grandparent black instead of red (breaking black-height), and rotates the wrong direction (breaking BST order, since this branch is reached when `parent` is the grandparent's *right* child — the fix-up needs a left rotation there, not right). It should read:

```c
// Case 3
problemNode->parent->color = RB_BLACK;
problemNode->parent->parent->color = RB_RED;
rb_rotate_left(t, problemNode->parent->parent);
```

This path is hit directly by the RR "line" shape (no Case 2 needed) and after Case 2 in the RL zigzag — which is exactly what test cases 7 (ascending run) and 10 (RL zigzag) in [tests/test_rbtree.c](tests/test_rbtree.c#L588) exercise, so both would fail/corrupt the tree as written.

`rb_rotate_left`/`rb_rotate_right` themselves ([src/rbtree.c:47-83](src/rbtree.c#L47-L83)) look correct — they match the draft and each other as proper mirrors. No syntax errors that would block compilation; both issues are logic/order-of-operations bugs.

